# builtins deliberately absent from the hand-written docs, with why. An entry
# whose builtin is documented later is an ERROR (scripts/doc_coverage_check.sh),
# so this stays a list of intentional omissions, not a to-do pile.

# The RV32I/RV64 backend's internal spellings. `args`, `time` and `random_int`
# are the portable names a program writes; these are what the prelude lowers
# them to on the -rv path, refused on every other backend. Documenting them
# would invite a program to branch on the target, which is the opposite of the
# point -- the portable name works everywhere and these do not.
__rv_argc     internal: what `args` lowers to on -rv (reads the loader's block)
__rv_argstr   internal: what `args` lowers to on -rv (one argument as a string)
__rv_clock    internal: what `time` lowers to on -rv (clock_gettime64)
__rv_urandom32 internal: what `random_int` draws from on -rv (getrandom)
__rv_xlen     internal: the machine word width, for the prelude's one width branch
__rv_str_hash internal: FNV-1a over a str's bytes, for the -rv prelude's hash-table Map
__rv_word     internal: a value's machine word, for backend diagnostics only

# v0.1.404's hosted file I/O on -rv, same rule: `read_file`, `write_file`,
# `file_exists`, `read_stdin` and `substring` are the portable names; these are
# what the prelude lowers them to (Linux openat/read/write/faccessat, and the
# raw substring behind substring's bounds check), refused everywhere else.
__rv_open_rd       internal: what `read_file` opens with on -rv (openat O_RDONLY)
__rv_read_all      internal: what `read_file` / `read_stdin` read with on -rv
__rv_access        internal: what `file_exists` asks on -rv (faccessat)
__rv_unlink        internal: what `file_delete` asks on -rv (unlinkat)
__rv_syscall       internal: the -rv prelude's Linux-numbered ecall (dir_create, dir_entries, dir_remove)
__rv_cstr          internal: a str with a NUL after it, for a path, on -rv
__rv_addr          internal: a str's own address, for a buffer the host fills, on -rv
__rv_envblk        internal: the environment block the host leaves on -rv (env_pairs, env_var)
__rv_open_wr       internal: what `write_file` opens with on -rv (openat O_WRONLY)
__rv_write_all     internal: what `write_file` writes with on -rv
__rv_substring_raw internal: the unchecked substring behind `substring`'s bounds refusal on -rv
__coro_new_raw     internal: what `coro_new` is rewritten into before type-checking -- the body is handed its own handle
__coro_msg         internal: the read of what was sent to a coroutine, in the body `coro_new` is rewritten into
__coro_new_sized_raw internal: what `coro_new_sized` is rewritten into before type-checking
__chan_recv_pair   internal: what `channel_recv_opt` is rewritten into on LLVM and Wasm -- the receive as a (got, value) pair
__chan_recv_pair_timeout internal: the same for `channel_recv_timeout`
__rv_alloc_keep    internal: the RV prelude's coroutine runtime (v0.1.623)
__rv_boot_addr     internal: the RV prelude's coroutine runtime (v0.1.623)
